/**
@see {@link ClearAreaLayout.clearAreaStrategy}
@final*/
export enum ClearAreaStrategy {
/**
A strategy that clears the area by trying to minimize the number of moved nodes, changing the layout rather locally.
From the specified
{@link ClearAreaLayout.area}, stripes running from the area border to the graph border in all four directions are considered. The area is now
cleared by simply moving all elements inside the stripe in that direction, as far as necessary. If elements are not
completely inside the stripe but collide with moved elements, then they are moved along too. This way, the moving stripe
can quickly grow in some scenarios.

All edges where at least one node was moved are finally rerouted by the {@link ClearAreaLayout.edgeRouter routing algorithm}. This also applies for edges
that cross the area if property {@link ClearAreaLayout.considerEdges} is enabled.

__Warning:__ This strategy does not consider the {@link ClearAreaLayout.componentAssignmentStrategy component assignment} setting.

__Warning:__ Specifying an {@link ClearAreaLayout.AREA_GROUP_NODE_DP_KEY area group node} and a {@link PartitionGrid} structure at the same time is not supported by this
strategy. In that case the area group node is ignored, i.e. the space will not be created inside any group but the
partition grid constraints are preserved. Therefore, it is strongly discouraged to select this strategy for grouped
graphs that have a {@link PartitionGrid}.

[Read this documentation online](https://docs.yworks.com/yfileshtml/#/api/ClearAreaStrategy%23LOCAL).

@see {@link ClearAreaLayout.clearAreaStrategy}*/
LOCAL,
/**
A strategy that clears the area by trying to minimize the number of moved nodes, changing the layout rather locally but
moving all necessary nodes by a uniform offset.
This strategy basically works like {@link ClearAreaStrategy.LOCAL} with the difference that nodes that are
required to move are moved uniformly, by the same location offset. Therefore, compared to {@link ClearAreaStrategy.LOCAL}
this strategy preserves more of the initial relations. On the other hand, results can become less compact. As nodes are
uniformly moved, edges where both end nodes were shifted do not need to be rerouted.

__Warning:__ This strategy does not consider the {@link ClearAreaLayout.componentAssignmentStrategy component assignment} setting.

__Warning:__ Specifying an {@link ClearAreaLayout.AREA_GROUP_NODE_DP_KEY area group node} and a {@link PartitionGrid} structure at the same time is not supported by this
strategy. In that case the area group node is ignored, i.e. the space will not be created inside any group but the
partition grid constraints are preserved. Therefore, it is strongly discouraged to select this strategy for grouped
graphs that have a {@link PartitionGrid}.

@see {@link ClearAreaLayout.clearAreaStrategy}*/
LOCAL_UNIFORM,
/**
A strategy that clears the area with the goal to preserve the shape of the existing edge paths, moving related elements
in common.
Preserving the shapes of the edge paths means that the direction of the edge segments is not changed and no new bends
are created. The segment lengths may change in order to generate free space. The effect of this strategy is that the
relative routes remain the same. The general aesthetics of a given layout are, ideally, preserved. The layout only
becomes sparser to free up the desired space.

This strategy models all graph elements (nodes, edges, bends, labels) the same way and unifies related elements where
appropriate. They are then moved in common in order to keep the relation among them as is.

## Note

Compared to the local approaches {@link ClearAreaStrategy.LOCAL} and
{@link ClearAreaStrategy.LOCAL_UNIFORM}, this strategy usually needs to move a larger number of nodes to achieve
its goal.

## Note

With this approach, the best results can be obtained for input graphs with orthogonal edge routes and where the distance
between elements does not fall below the specified {@link ClearAreaLayout.spacing spacing}.

[Read this documentation online](https://docs.yworks.com/yfileshtml/#/api/ClearAreaStrategy%23PRESERVE_SHAPES).

@see {@link ClearAreaLayout.clearAreaStrategy}*/
PRESERVE_SHAPES,
/**
A strategy that clears the area with the goal to preserve the shape of the existing edge paths, moving related elements
in common and also moving all elements by a uniform offset.
In addition to the related strategy
{@link ClearAreaStrategy.PRESERVE_SHAPES}, this one uses a uniform offset for all elements that are moved. This
way the relative locations between all elements in the graph are preserved. On the other hand, unnecessary additional
space may be generated.

Preserving the shapes of the edge paths means that the direction of the edge segments is not changed and no new bends
are created. The segment lengths may change in order to generate free space. The effect of this strategy is that the
relative routes remain the same. The general aesthetics of a given layout are, ideally, preserved. The layout only
becomes sparser to free up the desired space.

This strategy models all graph elements (nodes, edges, bends, labels) the same way and unifies related elements where
appropriate. They are then moved in common in order to keep the relation among them as is.

## Note

Compared to the local approaches {@link ClearAreaStrategy.LOCAL} and
{@link ClearAreaStrategy.LOCAL_UNIFORM}, this strategy usually needs to move a larger number of nodes to achieve
its goal.

## Note

With this approach, the best results can be obtained for input graphs with orthogonal edge routes and where the distance
between elements does not fall below the specified {@link ClearAreaLayout.spacing spacing}.

[Read this documentation online](https://docs.yworks.com/yfileshtml/#/api/ClearAreaStrategy%23PRESERVE_SHAPES_UNIFORM).

@see {@link ClearAreaLayout.clearAreaStrategy}*/
PRESERVE_SHAPES_UNIFORM,
/**
A strategy that clears the area by globally partitioning the input into two parts and moving them apart.
The existing layout is divided into two partitions somewhere along the {@link ClearAreaLayout.area area}. The division can be vertical or horizontal.
To free up the space, the two components are then moved horizontally (vertical division) or vertically (horizontal
division). Edges connecting nodes of different partitions are rerouted by the {@link ClearAreaLayout.edgeRouter routing algorithm}.

This strategy has a rather global effect on the existing layout because every element is displaced. The edge routes and
relative locations between elements that are in the same partition are not changed at all. Compared to other strategies,
more unnecessary free space may be generated as not only the exact {@link ClearAreaLayout.area area} is cleared, but a whole vertical or horizontal
stripe.

The specified {@link ClearAreaLayout.componentAssignmentStrategy component assignments} is considered such that if possible all component nodes are in the same partition.

@see {@link ClearAreaLayout.clearAreaStrategy}*/
GLOBAL
}
export namespace ClearAreaStrategy {
/**
Converts the argument to {@link ClearAreaStrategy}.
@param value The value to convert to {@link ClearAreaStrategy}.
@returns Either `value` if it is already a {@link ClearAreaStrategy} or an appropriate converted value.*/
function from(value: ClearAreaStrategy | ClearAreaStrategyStringValues): ClearAreaStrategy;
const $class: Class<ClearAreaStrategy>;
}
/** Automatic conversion to {@link ClearAreaStrategy}. */
type ClearAreaStrategyStringValues = "local" | "local-uniform" | "preserve-shapes" | "preserve-shapes-uniform" | "global";
