/**
Routing policies for edge routing algorithms to specify whether a new path is calculated in any case or if the existing
routes determine whether a new path is necessary.
@see {@link BusRouterBusDescriptor.BusDescriptor}
@see {@link BusRouterBusDescriptor.routingPolicy}
@see {@link ChannelEdgeRouter.routingPolicy}
@see {@link EdgeRouterEdgeLayoutDescriptor.routingPolicy}
@final*/
export enum RoutingPolicy {
/**
A routing policy that indicates that a new route is calculated in any case.
The existing path of an edge is ignored and the routing algorithm unconditionally generates a whole new path.*/
ALWAYS,
/**
A routing policy that indicates that based on the current path it is automatically determined whether an edge is routed,
and if so, the edge gets a whole new path.
The automatic selection examines the existing, given path of an edge. Based on various criteria it heuristically
determines whether or not it needs to be routed. For example, intersections with other elements or a routing style
violation are clear indicators. A further criterion is when the port constraints/candidates are not properly satisfied.
Edges deemed to be 'good' are not changed.

This policy is convenient for cases where it is not clear which edges the algorithm should route. For example, after a
user interaction (moving a node, inserting new elements etc.), routing of edges might become necessary but depending on
the action it can not definitely be said which edges need to be corrected.*/
PATH_AS_NEEDED,
/**
A routing policy that indicates that based on the current path it is automatically determined whether the edge is
routed, and if so, only the necessary segments are changed.
This policy follows the same approach as {@link RoutingPolicy.PATH_AS_NEEDED} for determining whether or not an
edge is even routed. However, during routing, the
_changes are kept local_. This means that only certain segments are changed, keeping the rest of the route, wherever appropriate. Edges deemed
to be 'good' are not changed at all.

This policy is convenient for cases where it is not clear which edges the algorithm should route. For example, after a
user interaction (moving a node, inserting new elements etc.), routing of edges might become necessary but depending on
the action it can not definitely be said which edges need to be corrected.

## Note

When having {@link EdgeRouterEdgeRoutingStyle.CURVED curved edges}, this policy is equal to
{@link RoutingPolicy.PATH_AS_NEEDED}, meaning that if necessary the whole curved edge will be routed.

[Read this documentation online](https://docs.yworks.com/yfileshtml/#/api/RoutingPolicy%23SEGMENTS_AS_NEEDED).*/
SEGMENTS_AS_NEEDED
}
