/**
A {@link ILayoutStage} that bundles the edges of general undirected graphs to avoid visual cluttering.
Bundling together multiple edges means that their common parts are to some degree merged into a bundled part, which is a
useful technique to increase the readability of straight-line graph drawings with a high number of edges that connect a
comparably small number of nodes. This stage provides means to generate bundled edge paths for any given layout, i.e.,
the layout generated by a specified
{@link LayoutStageBase.coreLayout core layout algorithm}. To determine whether two edges can be bundled, the algorithm computes a compatibility measure
that is composed of various criteria (e.g. edges that are closer together or have similar length are more compatible).
Note that this stage ignores the edge bends and handles them all as straight-lines. Furthermore, it doesn't prevent
overlaps between nodes and edges.

## Concept

The implemented algorithm is based on the description in: "Force-directed Edge Bundling for Graph Visualization" by
Danny Holten and Jarke J. van Wijk, in Computer Graphics Forum 28(3): 893-990, 2009.

The main idea of the algorithm is that the edges are subdivided into segments and between each pair of consecutive
subdivision points spring and electrostatic forces are exerted. At each iteration step, each subdivision point is moved
according to the combined force exerted on it. The algorithm runs in six cycles and starts with an initial number of
subdivision points for each edge and an initial step size that determines the distance to which each subdivision point
can be moved in the direction of the combined force (exerted on it) at each iteration step of the algorithm. After each
cycle run, the value of the step size is halved.

## Features

Global settings of the bundling can be configured using an {@link EdgeBundling} instance. In particular, it is
possible to determine which of the edges are compatible and thus, can be bundled together by specifying the strength of
the edge bundles using the {@link EdgeBundling.bundlingStrength} property. For the particular algorithm, the default value for this property is `0.4`. The {@link EdgeBundling.bundlingQuality bundling quality}
is related to the distance to which a subdivision point can be moved at each iteration step of the algorithm. The
default value is `1`.

The individual style of each edge can be configured using an {@link EdgeBundleDescriptor}. A {@link IDataProvider}
can be registered with the input graph with key {@link EdgeBundling.EDGE_BUNDLE_DESCRIPTOR_DP_KEY} to assign
descriptors to edges. This allows, for example, to define which edges should actually be bundled.

To visualize better the result of the edge bundling algorithm, it is recommended that the edges are rendered using an
opacity factor. In this manner, edges that are strongly bundled together will be emphasized more, in comparison to
others that are not bundled.

## Note

This algorithm doesn't consider the current bends of the edges, i.e., it always handles them as straight-line.
Furthermore, this stage doesn't prevent overlaps between nodes and edges.

__Warning:__ Using high values of for the {@link EdgeBundling.bundlingStrength bundling strength} can significantly affect the performance of the algorithm, especially
for large graphs. A value close to `0.4` is recommended. On the other hand, using zero values for the {@link EdgeBundling.bundlingStrength bundling strength} or
the {@link EdgeBundling.bundlingQuality bundling quality} leads to straight-line edges.

__Warning:__ The bundle descriptor property {@link EdgeBundleDescriptor.considerDirection} is ignored by this stage.

[Read this documentation online](https://docs.yworks.com/yfileshtml/#/api/EdgeBundlingStage).*/
export class EdgeBundlingStage {
/**
Creates a new {@link EdgeBundlingStage} instance with the given optional core layout algorithm and default
settings.
@param [core=null] the core layout algorithm*/
constructor(core?: ILayoutAlgorithm | null);
/**
Applies the edge bundling algorithm after invoking the {@link LayoutStageBase.coreLayout core layout algorithm}.
@param graph the input graph*/
applyLayout(graph: LayoutGraph): void;
/**
Gets or sets the maximum duration in milliseconds that this bundling algorithm is allowed to run.
The duration needs to be non-negative.

## Note

Restricting the maximum duration may result in a lower bundling quality. Furthermore, the real runtime may exceed the
maximum duration since the algorithm still has to find a valid solution.

@throws {@link Exception} __(ArgumentError)__ if the specified duration has a negative value*/
get maximumDuration(): number;
set maximumDuration(value: number);
/**
Gets the {@link EdgeBundling} instance that defines the settings of the edge bundling feature.
The specified {@link EdgeBundling} defines global bundling properties. Settings for individual edges can be
defined by assigning an {@link EdgeBundleDescriptor} to an edge using a {@link IDataProvider} registered
with key {@link EdgeBundling.EDGE_BUNDLE_DESCRIPTOR_DP_KEY}.

By default, all edges are bundled (property {@link EdgeBundleDescriptor.bundled} of the default bundle descriptor is enabled). The default for {@link EdgeBundling.bundlingStrength} is `0.4` and
the default for property {@link EdgeBundling.bundlingQuality} is `1.0`.*/
get edgeBundling(): EdgeBundling;
static $class: Class<EdgeBundlingStage>;
private $_EdgeBundlingStage: 0;
/**
Whether the given object is an instance of this type.
@param o The object to test.*/
static isInstance(o: any): o is EdgeBundlingStage;
}
export interface EdgeBundlingStage extends LayoutStageBase {
}
/**
This edge routing algorithm applies organic routes to the edges of the graph.
## Layout Style

Edges are routed organically, i.e. in smooth curves around the nodes observing a {@link OrganicEdgeRouter.minimumDistance minimum distance} to the nodes.

During the routing process, the positions of the nodes are considered to be fixed and the router will not modify their
locations or their sizes in any way.

The edge routing algorithm can be applied whenever edge paths should avoid crossing any nodes in organic or cyclic
layout styles.

## Concept

The edge routing algorithm uses a force-directed approach to calculate the edge paths. Nodes are considered to be
repulsive forces while edges will try to become as short as possible.

Each edge is routed separately and is influenced by the nodes in a certain area around it. The algorithm will add bends
to the edge path that are placed by balancing the forces.

The quality of the result highly depends on how much space there is between the nodes. More precisely, the distance
between each pair of nodes should be at least twice the specified {@link OrganicEdgeRouter.minimumDistance minimum distance}. If it is not necessary that the
nodes keep their locations, this can be ensured using a combination of {@link RemoveOverlapsStage} and
{@link OrganicEdgeRouter.createNodeEnlargementStage node enlargement stage}.

## Features

{@link OrganicEdgeRouter.minimumDistance} will make edges keep a custom distance to the nodes. However, if there is not enough space between the nodes, this
distance may be undershot (i.e. edges will be closer to nodes).

{@link OrganicEdgeRouter} is able to {@link OrganicEdgeRouter.keepExistingBends reuse existing bends}. Edges will contain those bends along with other bends
added by the layout algorithm. Another feature allows to consider bends such that they influence the route finding,
however, their absolute location is not kept (see {@link OrganicEdgeRouter.considerExistingBends}).

This edge routing algorithm is realized as a {@link ILayoutStage} which can be applied to a graph directly or
using a {@link OrganicEdgeRouter.coreLayout core layout algorithm}.

[Read this documentation online](https://docs.yworks.com/yfileshtml/#/api/OrganicEdgeRouter).*/
