/**
This layout algorithm arranges graphs with a tree structure.
## Layout Style

{@link ClassicTreeLayout} is designed to arrange directed and undirected trees that have a unique root node. All
children are placed below their parent in relation to the main layout direction. The edges of the graph are routed as
straight-line segments or in an orthogonal bus-like fashion.

Tree layout algorithms are commonly used for visualizing relational data and for producing diagrams of high quality that
are able to reveal possible hierarchic properties of the graph. More precisely, they find applications in dataflow
analysis, software engineering, bioinformatics and business administration.

## Concept

The layout algorithm starts from the root and recursively assigns coordinates to all tree nodes. In this manner, leaf
nodes will be placed first, while each parent node is placed centered above its children.

## Features

The layout algorithm supports the custom sorting of sibling nodes (i.e., nodes with the same parent node). The property
{@link ClassicTreeLayout.comparer} allows specifying the {@link IComparer} instance that is used to sort the outgoing edges of each node of the tree. By default, the
algorithm sorts the edges by the x-coordinate of the associated target node in ascending order. If the {@link IComparer} is set to an
instance of {@link NodeOrderComparer}, the outgoing edges are sorted by the {@link IComparable} instances
associated with the edges' target. The mapping of nodes to {@link IComparable}s can be specified by a {@link IDataProvider}
that is registered to the graph with key {@link NodeOrderComparer.NODE_ORDER_COMPARABLE_DP_KEY}.

A custom node can be defined as root of the tree using a {@link IDataProvider} registered with the graph with
key {@link ClassicTreeLayout.SELECTED_ROOT_DP_KEY}.

{@link ClassicTreeLayout} can be configured to {@link ClassicTreeLayout.considerNodeLabels reserve space for node labels}. It can also {@link ClassicTreeLayout.integratedEdgeLabeling place edge labels along edges}
such that the labels won't overlap with other graph elements. Edge labels are placed according to the information stored
in a {@link PreferredPlacementDescriptor} instance. However, the placement along the edge will only affect the
order of multiple labels at the same edge. The algorithm will always place the labels close to the target node.

Grouping of nodes can also be handled by this layout algorithm. It is important that a group node contains a whole
subtree. Otherwise, the group nodes may overlap with each other or with other nodes. Furthermore, the user may specify
minimum size constraints for each group node using {@link IDataProvider} key
{@link GroupingKeys.MINIMUM_NODE_SIZE_DP_KEY}.

This layout algorithm can only handle graphs with a tree structure. To apply it to a general graph, a {@link TreeReductionStage}
can be appended. This stage will temporarily remove some edges of the input graph until a tree is obtained. After the
layout calculation, the stage will reinsert the edges that were removed and route them separately.

[Read this documentation online](https://docs.yworks.com/yfileshtml/#/api/ClassicTreeLayout).*/
